<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Eleanor of England, Queen of Castile</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1162">1162</a>–<a href="/years/1214">1214</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Leonor of England · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-plantagenet">House of Plantagenet</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Eleanor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> was a daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-ii-of-england">Henry II of England</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eleanor-of-aquitaine">Eleanor of Aquitaine</a> who married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-viii">Alfonso VIII</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> in 1177. She was a patron of religious houses, including the royal convent of Las Huelgas at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgos">Burgos</a>, and of troubadours. Her children included Berengaria, later queen of Castile, Henry I of Castile and Blanche, queen of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. She died a few weeks after her husband in 1214.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/eleanor-of-england-queen-of-castile.jpg" alt="Eleanor of England, Queen of Castile" width="186" height="462" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:EleonoraAngl.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Eleanor was born in the castle at Domfront in Normandy, the sixth child and second daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-ii-of-england">Henry II of England</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eleanor-of-aquitaine">Eleanor of Aquitaine</a>, after whom she was named. Her full siblings included Henry the Young King, Duchess Matilda of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-i-of-england">Richard I</a>, Geoffrey II of Brittany, Queen Joan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> and John. In 1165 her marriage was arranged to Frederick V, Duke of Swabia, eldest son of the Holy Roman Emperor Frederick I Barbarossa, but he died before it could take place.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Her marriage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-viii">Alfonso VIII</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> was meant to secure Aquitaine's Pyrenean border for her parents, while Alfonso sought an ally against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sancho-vi-of-navarre">Sancho VI of Navarre</a>. Her marriage treaty gave her direct control of lands, towns and castles across the kingdom, among them Aguilar de Campóo, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/logrono">Logroño</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calahorra">Calahorra</a>. Of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eleanor-of-aquitaine">Eleanor of Aquitaine</a>'s daughters, she alone could wield influence like her mother's. Alfonso's will of 1204 provided that she would rule alongside their son if he died, and she persuaded him to marry their daughter Berengaria to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-ix-of-leon">Alfonso IX of León</a>.</p><p>Around 1200 Alfonso began to claim that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gascony">Gascony</a> had been part of her dowry, though there is no documented foundation for the claim. He invaded it in her name in 1205 and gave up the claim in 1208. Her patronage drew troubadours and sages to the court; the Catalan poet Ramon Vidal de Besalu wrote about her, and Guillem de Bergued addressed a poem to her. In 1179 she took on the upkeep of the shrine of St <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-becket">Thomas Becket</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toledo">Toledo</a> cathedral.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Eleanor founded and supported the Abbey of Santa María la Real de Las Huelgas outside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgos">Burgos</a> and its hospital for pilgrims, which served as a refuge and tomb for her family for generations. Her daughter Constance took the veil there. When Alfonso died in 1214 she was reportedly too grieved to preside at the burial, and Berengaria performed the honours. By his will she became regent for her son Henry I, with Berengaria as adviser. In poor health, she left most affairs to her daughter, and nobles feared she meant to hand over the regency. She died twenty-six days after her husband and was buried at Las Huelgas. Her great-grandson Alfonso X called her noble and much loved.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Eleanor of England, Queen of Castile” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Domfront, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgos">Burgos</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=48.59,-0.64&amp;year=1195" aria-label="Domfront on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4535 466 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Eleanor of England, Queen of Castile"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4985 736h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4985 736h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=48.59,-0.64&amp;year=1195">What was here in 1195? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
